{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"resentful-titanite-viper","creation_channel":"cli","description":"## Context / Problem\nResearch handoff, not a claim on the work: **a parallel session currently owns CI performance,\nthe Python-version matrix and 3.14 adoption.** This task records a comparison of rebar's Gerrit\nVerified gate against five actively-maintained Python projects (Django, Pydantic, pytest,\nurllib3, cryptography — all with commits in the 10–22 Aug 2026 window) so the findings are not\nlost, and so the owner can take what fits.\n\n**Verified against current `origin/main`, not the older commit the study cited:**\n- `requires-python = \">=3.11\"`, but the blocking matrix stops at **3.13**\n  (`_build-and-test.yml:307-315`: `ubuntu-latest`/`macos-latest` × 3.11/3.12/3.13, with macOS\n  3.11 and 3.12 excluded — so macOS runs 3.13 only, giving 4 cells).\n- `codeql.yml:20-26` triggers on **`schedule` + `workflow_dispatch` only** — no\n  `pull_request`/`workflow_call`, so CodeQL structurally CANNOT gate a Gerrit merge.\n- Zizmor is scoped to `release.yml` (`Makefile:243-244`, which says widening it \"is separate\n  work\"); actionlint does cover all workflows, but it checks syntax, not supply chain.\n\n## What rebar already matches or exceeds\nWorth stating so this is not read as a deficit list: exact Gerrit patchset checkout with\nfail-closed voting, ruff/mypy/pre-commit/ShellCheck/actionlint/lockfile validation,\nseverity-aware dependency audit, four test tiers, per-test and whole-job timeouts, targeted\nmutation testing, per-extra and no-extra install isolation, wheel/sdist provenance probes,\nDependabot, trusted publishing, PEP 740 attestations, and documentation/schema/complexity/\nmodule-size drift gates. Several of these exceed the comparison set.\n\n## Approved direction (from the CI owner)\n- Gerrit Verified gate tests the **oldest and newest supported** versions; intermediate\n  versions move to the **scheduled sweep against main**.\n- **macOS stays on the hot path but scoped to compatibility tests**; Ubuntu runs the full\n  suite.\n- **Windows is a candidate for the scheduled sweep** — coverage without hot-path latency.\n\n## Findings, ordered by value per minute of added latency\n**Zero added hot-path latency:**\n1. **Widen Zizmor beyond `release.yml`.** It already runs under `make lint`; the scope is a\n   Makefile argument. Gerrit Verified holds the SSH credential that casts the authoritative\n   vote, so its own workflow is the one most worth auditing.\n2. **SHA-pin third-party actions.** `gerrit-verify.yaml` states actions are SHA-pinned while\n   using mutable `@v7` refs; `_build-and-test.yml` uses `actions/checkout@v7` and\n   `actions/setup-python@v7`. Either pin them or correct the comment — a false claim in a\n   security-relevant file is worse than an accurate absence.\n3. **`twine check dist/*` in `_artifact-probe.yml`.** The probe already builds and installs\n   distributions; metadata validation currently waits until release. Seconds, pre-merge.\n\n**Scheduled sweep, no hot-path cost:** intermediate Python versions (3.12/3.13), Windows,\n3.15-dev and free-threaded 3.14t as non-blocking, dependency lower-bound resolution (the lock\nproves ONE resolution works, not that the declared floors do), external documentation link\nchecking, and the full macOS suite.\n\n**Needs a latency decision, so flagged rather than recommended:** running CodeQL on the exact\nGerrit refspec and adding it to `vote.needs`. Today a CodeQL-detectable defect can earn\n`Verified +1`. urllib3 runs it per-PR. This is the one item that genuinely adds pre-merge\nminutes; whether that trade is worth it is the owner's call, not this ticket's.\n\n**Deferred with reasoning:** raising the 70% branch-coverage floor toward the 100% that urllib3\nand cryptography enforce. rebar's targeted mutation testing is stronger evidence than raw\ncoverage, and forcing a global percentage buys low-value tests. Diff coverage on changed lines\nis the better first step. Downstream-consumer testing is premature until public integrations\nexist.\n\n## Scope\nNo files. This is a research record for the CI owner; any implementation belongs in tickets\nthey raise, sized against their own latency budget.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] [non-codebase] The CI owner has seen this record and each finding is dispositioned —\n      adopted, deferred with a reason, or rejected with a reason; checked by a comment here\n      naming the disposition of each numbered item.\n- [ ] [non-codebase] Any adopted item exists as its own ticket with its own acceptance\n      criteria; checked by links from this ticket.","id":"81fd-c98b-9ca2-4a73","parent_id":"","priority":2,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"task","title":"OSS CI-practice comparison: research handoff for the CI owner"},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1787703171441141001,"uuid":"95ab8f84-7813-43cb-9f70-749b6d1d276c"}